{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-331026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-14)({t}^{2}-5t+4)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-14)({t}^{2}-5t+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=14,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-14)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-14s}\\,L{g(t+14)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-5t+4.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+14) & =(t+14)^{2}-5(t+14)+4 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+28t+196\\,-\\,5t-70\\,+\\,4 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+23t+130.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+14)} & =L{{t}^{2}+23t+130} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+23L{t}+130L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+23\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+130\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-14)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-14s}\\,L{g(t+14)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}})"] ["math" "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-5)({t}^{2}+4t+1)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)({t}^{2}+4t+1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+4t+1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =(t+5)^{2}+4(t+5)+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+10t+25\\,+\\,4t+20\\,+\\,1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+5)} & =L{{t}^{2}+14t+46} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+14L{t}+46L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+46\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-5)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-5s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}})"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-6)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-6s}\\,L{g(t+6)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-t+3.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =(t+6)^{2}-(t+6)+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+12t+36\\,-\\,t-6\\,+\\,3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+11t+33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+6)} & =L{{t}^{2}+11t+33} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+11L{t}+33L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+11\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+33\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-6)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L{g(t+6)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})"] ["math" "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-11)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-11s}\\,L{g(t+11)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-2t+5.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+11) & =(t+11)^{2}-2(t+11)+5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+22t+121\\,-\\,2t-22\\,+\\,5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+20t+104.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+11)} & =L{{t}^{2}+20t+104} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+20L{t}+104L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+20\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+104\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-11)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-11s}\\,L{g(t+11)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})"] ["math" "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-8)({t}^{2}+5t-1)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-8)({t}^{2}+5t-1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=8,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-8)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-8s}\\,L{g(t+8)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+5t-1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+8) & =(t+8)^{2}+5(t+8)-1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+16t+64\\,+\\,5t+40\\,-\\,1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+21t+103.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+8)} & =L{{t}^{2}+21t+103} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+21L{t}+103L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+21\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+103\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-8)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-8s}\\,L{g(t+8)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{-1}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}})"] ["math" "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s})"] ["math" "{e}^{8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-1)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+5\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot (-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+1)} & =L{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+20\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡20\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡20\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+3) & =cos\\,(\\pi (t+3)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+3)} & =L{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+3) & =cos\\,(6\\pi (t+3)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t+18\\pi ) \\\\  & =cos\\,(6\\pi t+9\\cdot 2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+3)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+(6\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi }{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi }{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =sin\\,(\\pi (t+2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-331040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-331040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3))},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)=sin\\,(5(t-3)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}=\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3))} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-342637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+9}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}"] ["math" "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $L{u(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)={e}^{-2(t-3)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-342318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}"] ["math" "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-5)cos\\,(4(t-5))},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-5)cos\\,(4(t-5)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=5.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-5)=cos\\,(4(t-5)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-5)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-5s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-5)cos\\,(4(t-5))} & ={e}^{-5s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-342631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}"] ["math" "{e}^{5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-4)sin\\,(2(t-4))},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-4)sin\\,(2(t-4)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=4.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-4)=sin\\,(2(t-4)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-4)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-4s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)sin\\,(2(t-4))} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-342638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "{e}^{4s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=4.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-4)={e}^{-5(t-4)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-4)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-4s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "63b797a103323b82c6ee7c59cc26d8ccb01dc4e6ca4aa4dd3553635809bb9c63"} {"question" "q-342626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5}"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}"] ["math" "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "q-342626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f10b3d3cba1c6d4e43686fa743b5251e2894aa310d395e2c3b2dc0d0ffffba8"} {"question" "e-21445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/example-21445.json" "file_sha256" "860f98d4a18f9f3348ab719763c18bc546c798a5ef9e378b47eb93dbdbe36aea"} {"question" "e-21445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=t^2+3t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =(t+2)^{2}+3(t+2)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t+4\\,+\\,3t+6\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+7t+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{{t}^{2}+7t+12} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+7L{t}+12L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+12\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/example-21445.json" "file_sha256" "860f98d4a18f9f3348ab719763c18bc546c798a5ef9e378b47eb93dbdbe36aea"} {"question" "e-21582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/example-21582.json" "file_sha256" "05bbd51822664fb2e233159c0bb53e3bb246ce74bff952c14a54873882f5b901"} {"question" "e-21582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(9\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t+36\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡36\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡36\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/example-21582.json" "file_sha256" "05bbd51822664fb2e233159c0bb53e3bb246ce74bff952c14a54873882f5b901"} {"question" "e-21864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-1)(t-1)^3 \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/example-21864.json" "file_sha256" "4517adf880ee57bf24942bb8687d2f09c52c524ac90dc144e9aca01f34cfa250"} {"question" "e-21864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-1)(t-1)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a = 1.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1) = (t-1)^3.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t{:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = t^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}{:}$\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^3\\} = \\dfrac{3!}{s^{3+1}} = \\dfrac{6}{s^4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor $e^{-s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)(t-1)^{3}} & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072754/example-21864.json" "file_sha256" "4517adf880ee57bf24942bb8687d2f09c52c524ac90dc144e9aca01f34cfa250"}] "usage" {"e-21582" [] "e-21864" [] "q-342318" [] "q-331016" [] "e-21445" []} "basis" 2652 "retractions" [[17592186221878 :question/problem "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$ for $s > 0$, find $L{u(t - 5)(t^{2} + 4t + 1)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186221969 :question/problem "Find $L{u(t - 3)e^{-2(t - 3)}}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186221097 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186221097 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\}, \\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(t)=t^2+3t+2\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t + 2) &= (t + 2)^{2} + 3(t + 2) + 2 \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 4t + 4 + 3t + 6 + 2 \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 7t + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}\n$$\n\nfor $s>0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t + 2)} &= L{t^{2} + 7t + 12} \\\\\n&= L{t^{2}} + 7L{t} + 12L{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{7}{s^{2}} + \\frac{12}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 2)g(t)} &= e^{-2s}L{g(t + 2)} \\\\\n&= e^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{7}{s^{2}} + \\frac{12}{s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221107 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186221107 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\}, \\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t + 4) &= \\cos(9π(t + 4)) \\\\\n&= \\cos (9πt + 36π) \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt\\cos 36π - \\sin 9πt\\sin 36π \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt \\cdot 1 - \\sin 9πt \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}\n$$\n\nfor $s>0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t + 4)} &= L{\\cos (9πt)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 4)g(t)} &= e^{-4s}L{g(t + 4)} \\\\\n&= e^{-4s} \\cdot \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221146 :question/problem "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-1)(t-1)^3 \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186221146 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n$u(t-1)(t-1)^3$.\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a = 1$.\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1) = (t-1)^3$.\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t{:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = t^3\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^3\\} = \\dfrac{3!}{s^{3+1}} = \\dfrac{6}{s^4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nby multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor $e^{-s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 1)(t - 1)^{3}} &= e^{-s} \\cdot F(s) \\\\\n&= e^{-s} \\cdot \\frac{6}{s^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220170 17592186220171 17592186220172 17592186220173 17592186220174 17592186220809 17592186220810 17592186220811 17592186220812 17592186220813] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220169 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220169 :answer-field/id] [17592186220169 :answer-field/key] [17592186220169 :answer-field/type] [17592186220808 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220808 :answer-field/id] [17592186220808 :answer-field/key] [17592186220808 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
